
########## quaternion.py ##########

Sec. 2.1, the 4x4 matrix
  M(q) M(r) = M(qr) over 5000 random pairs
    max absolute error            1.78e-15
    relative to entry scale       1.51e-16
  -> correct.  This is the left regular representation of H, and matrix
     multiplication reproduces the Hamilton product exactly.  The claim
     'isomorphic 4x4 real matrix' holds as written.

  state carried by the node      4 real numbers
  slots the matrix occupies      16
  redundancy                     4 x

Sec. 2.1, 'concatenating three such state matrices'
  product equals blockwise (q_i r_i), max error  4.44e-16
  perturbing block 0 of the operand moves block 0 by  1.940
  and everything outside block 0 by                   0.000

  structurally zero entries      96 of 144  (67 %)
  nonzero entries in one node    48 of 144
  entries coupling block i to j  0
  state carried by the node      12 real numbers
  slots the matrix occupies      144
  redundancy                     12 x
  -> the algebra is H (+) H (+) H.  The three quaternions cannot exchange
     information, so the 12x12 node is three independent 4D systems in a
     container 12 times larger than their state, not a unified field.

Where does 12 come from?
  Sec. 2.1   3 quaternions x 4 components                  = 12
  Sec. 6     dim U(1) + dim SU(2) + dim SU(3) = 1 + 3 + 8  = 12
  -> both appear in the paper and they are not the same 12: the first is
     three copies of a 4D algebra, the second is the Lie algebra of a
     12-dimensional group with no quaternionic block structure.  Nothing
     in the paper connects them, and 112 is given no derivation at all
     (112 = 28 x 4 = 14 x 8, neither of which the paper mentions).

########## octonion.py ##########

Associators of the published multiplication table
  basis triples (i,j,k) with [e_i,e_j,e_k] != 0   168 of 512
  rank of the span of those associators           7
  dim Im O                                        7
  real part reached by any associator             0.0e+00
  -> the associators span Im O exactly, and never touch the real axis.

Consequence for any matrix form
  Matrix multiplication is associative, so an algebra homomorphism
  phi: O -> M_n(R) obeys phi((xy)z) = phi(x(yz)), i.e. phi([x,y,z]) = 0.
  The associators span a 7-dimensional subspace, so ker phi contains
  all of Im O and phi retains 1 of 8 dimensions (12.5 %).
  O is a simple algebra, so the only homomorphism into matrices is the
  zero map.  'Octonion algebra as a block matrix' is not an engineering
  problem; it has no solution at any n.

Left regular representation, 5000 random pairs each
  algebra      max |L_x L_y - L_xy|     relative
  H  (4x4)                 1.78e-15     4.24e-16
  O  (8x8)                 2.95e+01     2.76e+00
  -> the identical construction is exact for H and wrong by order 1 for O.

  L_x L_x vs L_(xx) on basis units (alternativity)  0.0e+00
  L_x L_y vs L_(xy) for x != y                      2.0e+00
  -> O is alternative, so squares still work; every mixed product does not.
     An MMA pipeline computes mixed products, which is the whole point of
     a 'unified' node, so the failure is exactly where the paper needs it
     to succeed.

########## symplectic.py ##########

1. Harmonic oscillator, 100,000 steps, dt = 0.01, E(0) = 0.500000
   update rule                                 E(end)         E/E0
   leapfrog, no training                     0.499991            1
   W in Sp(2), sigma = id,   b = 0                0.5            1
   W in Sp(2), sigma = id,   b = 1e-3        0.547046         1.09
   W in Sp(2), sigma = tanh, b = 0        1.00007e-05        2e-05
   W in Sp(2), sigma = relu, b = 0        2.26962e-05     4.54e-05
   -> the only row that conserves is the one where sigma is the identity
      and b is zero, i.e. where the update is a linear symplectic map and
      not a neural network layer at all.  tanh loses five orders of
      magnitude of energy; a bias of 1e-3 gains 9 %.

2. How large is the symplectic condition?
      2n  dim GL(2n)  dim Sp(2n)  constraints  fraction
      12         144          78           66    45.8%
     112       12544        6328         6216    49.6%
   a random 112x112 W misses the condition by max |W^T J W - J| = 1.186
   -> at 112x112, 6216 of 12544 degrees of freedom must satisfy equalities
      the paper never writes down.  Sp(112) is measure zero in GL(112).

3. Train W and b on exact data, then roll out

   harmonic (linear), E(0) = 1.125000
     rule                        1-step err  |W^T J W - J|       E(1e5)       E/E0
     leapfrog, no training                -              -      1.12498     1.0000
     sigma(Wx+b), sigma = id       4.44e-16       3.33e-16      1.12498     1.0000
     sigma(Wx+b), sigma = tanh     4.43e-02       2.69e-01      2.73851     2.4342

   pendulum (nonlinear), E(0) = 1.416147
     rule                        1-step err  |W^T J W - J|       E(1e5)       E/E0
     leapfrog, no training                -              -      1.41612     1.0000
     sigma(Wx+b), sigma = id       1.50e-03       2.49e-05      14.4747    10.2212
     sigma(Wx+b), sigma = tanh     4.44e-02       2.69e-01      2.46981     1.7440

   -> for the harmonic oscillator the true one-step map IS linear and
      symplectic, so least squares recovers it to 4e-16 and energy is
      conserved.  That is the special case, not the architecture working:
      add the nonlinearity the paper specifies and the same system gains a
      factor 2.4.  For the pendulum nothing conserves.  The linear fit is
      accurate to 1.5e-3 after a single step, which is a good fit, and
      ends ten times too energetic; the tanh fit lands at 1.74 x.  Neither
      number is a correction loop away from 1.0000.  Leapfrog, with no
      weights and no training, holds both to 5 decimal places, because
      conservation is a property of the structure of the update and
      sigma(W x + b) does not have that structure for any W and b.

########## roofline.py ##########

1. What a node stores
   112x112 fp16 block matrix              24.5 KiB   <- 'tens of kilobytes', consistent
   the 112 physical potentials           0.219 KiB
   overhead of the matrix form             112 x
   nonzero fraction if built from 28 quaternion 4x4 blocks    3.57%

2. The matrix form buys no arithmetic intensity
   operation                          FLOP     bytes  FLOP/byte
   W @ S  (112x112 node)         2,809,856    50,176       56.0
   W @ s  (112-vector)              25,088       448       56.0
   RTX 3090 ridge point 152 FLOP/byte -> both are memory bound
   -> identical position on the roofline, 112 x the FLOPs
      and 112 x the traffic.  The block matrix is overhead,
      not a path to the tensor cores.

3. 'executed in a single clock cycle'
   whole-chip fp16 throughput             83,529 FLOP/cycle
   one node's W @ S                    2,809,856 FLOP
   cycles, entire GPU on one node           33.6
   and if (x) is read as written, a Kronecker product of two 112x112
   matrices has 157,351,936 entries = 300 MiB per node.

4. Resident nodes and the step rate
        grid         nodes       S,S+1  x 24 GB  cards   s/step      Hz
       32^3        32,768      1.5 GiB     0.1x      1    0.002   569.3
       64^3       262,144     12.2 GiB     0.5x      1    0.014    71.2
       80^3       512,000     23.9 GiB     1.0x      1    0.027    36.4
      100^3     1,000,000     46.7 GiB     1.9x      2    0.054    18.7
      128^3     2,097,152     98.0 GiB     4.1x      5    0.112     8.9
      256^3    16,777,216    784.0 GiB    32.7x     33    0.899     1.1

   nodes whose state alone fills 24 GB          513,588  (80^3)
   nodes the bandwidth allows at 60 Hz          310,906  (68^3)
   -> 'millions of nodes' needs 47 GiB per million for state alone, with
      no weight matrices, no framebuffer and no rendering.  One card runs
      a 68^3 grid at 60 Hz with zero pixels drawn.

5. 'Tensor Core load in the efficient 15-20% range'
   at 15% load the tensor cores deliver   21.3 TFLOP/s and  120.7 TFLOP/s sit idle
   at 20% load the tensor cores deliver   28.4 TFLOP/s and  113.6 TFLOP/s sit idle
   -> 15-20 % utilisation of the only unit the architecture depends on is
      the symptom, not a design target.  The paper moves all physics onto
      the tensor cores precisely so they are saturated; a pipeline that
      leaves 80-85 % of them idle has not achieved that.  Item 2 above
      gives the reason: at 56 FLOP/byte the node is memory bound, so the
      tensor cores wait on VRAM no matter how the work is scheduled.

6. 'molecular brute-force simulation' at one node per atom
   atoms resident in 24 GB               513,588
   as a cube of crystalline silicon         21.7 nm on a side
   atoms in one mole                    6.02e+23
   cards for one mole of nodes          1.17e+18
   -> a 22 nm nanoparticle, state only, nothing else resident.  That is a
      legitimate size for atomistic work, which is why the comparison
      matters: molecular dynamics reaches it today with a few tens of
      bytes per atom, not 24.5 KiB, and without claiming 112 dimensions.
